5. saha-chara: false view by no true view.
The objects of pramdna are sémanya, common
qualities, i.e. generic attributes; or visesha, distinguishing
attributes, i.e. differentia. This twofold distinction is

- apphed to substances, attributes, and modifications.

Pramanabhasa (Fallacy)

Modes of acquiring knowledge, which look like
pramadna, but are not really so. They are:
1. a-sva-samvidita: knowledge by which the self
cannot be known, e.g. the Naiyayika system ;
2. grihttartha: knowing what is already known ;
dhardvahi-jnaina, e.g. it is a jug, it is a jug, it is a jug.
This does not add to our knowledge; what is not
known before (apirvartha) is what-we must know ;
3. nirvikalpa-dargana : intuitive perception. This
cannot be true pramdna ;
4. samsaya: doubtful or ambiguous knowledge
cannot be pramdna ; e.g. Is it a tree-trunk or a man? ;
5. viparyaya-jfidna: perverted knowledge cannot
be true pramdana ;
6. anadhyavasdya-jnana: uncertainty ; e.g. treading
a twig under foot, and saying : let it be;
7. pratyakshabhasa: misleading appearance; e.g,
something appears to the senses to be A, but really is

not A; as a mirage ;

